@noindent
The environment is essential to the evaluation process, because it determines the
context in which an expression should be evaluated.  Indeed, one could say that
expressions in a programming language do not, in themselves, have any meaning.
Rather, an expression acquires a meaning only with respect to some environment
in which it is evaluated.  Even the interpretation of an expression as
straightforward as @code{1 + 1} depends on an understanding that one is
operating in a context in which @code{+} is the operator for addition.  Thus, in
our model of evaluation we will always speak of evaluating an expression with
respect to some environment.  To describe programs, we will suppose that there
is a global environment, consisting of a single frame (with no enclosing
environment) that holds the program's top-level bindings.  In TypeScript this
frame is a module's collection of top-level declarations, and the names in it
are resolved when the program is loaded; the frames this chapter is about are
the ones a call opens while the program runs, and they hold parameters, local
bindings, and the cells that captured bindings keep alive.  For example, the
idea that @code{+} is the operator for addition needs no binding at all: the
operator belongs to the language itself, outside every frame, and what an
environment supplies is the meaning of each name that a procedure body merely
uses.  Chapter 4's evaluator gives this model a concrete data structure---a
chain of frames, each a table of bindings plus a pointer to the frame it
extends---and every captured cell in this section is what that structure
realizes.

