          | other variables                    |
global -->|                                    |
env       | square: --+                        |
          +-----------|---------------------+--+
                      |       ^             ^
square(5)             |       |             |
                      V       |             |
                  .---.---.   |         +---+--+
                  | O | O-+---+   E1 -->| x: 5 |
                  `-|-^---'             +------+
                    |
                    V
                  parameters: x
                  body: x·x
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.3b,,129mm,Figure 3.3: Environment created by evaluating square(5) in the global environment.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.3b.std,147mm,,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.3:} Environment created by evaluating @code{square(5)} in the global environment.}
@end float
@noindent
The environment model of procedure application can be summarized by two rules:

@itemize @bullet

@item
A procedure object is applied to a set of arguments by constructing a frame,
binding the formal parameters of the procedure to the arguments of the call,
and then evaluating the body of the procedure in the context of the new
environment constructed.  The new frame has as its enclosing environment the
environment part of the procedure object being applied.  In TypeScript this
frame is the call frame, and the environment part is the set of bindings the
function value captured where it was created.

@item
A procedure is created by evaluating a function expression relative to a
given environment.  The resulting procedure object is a pair consisting of the
text of the function expression and the captured bindings, which are the
pointer to the environment in which the procedure was created.

@end itemize

@noindent
We also specify that binding a name with @code{const} or @code{let} creates a
binding in the current environment frame and gives it the indicated
value.@footnote{If there is already a binding for the variable in the current
frame, TypeScript resolves the matter differently from the book's
@code{define}: a second @code{const} of the same name in one scope is a syntax
error, and changing the value of an existing binding requires the binding to
have been declared with @code{let} or to hold a cell.  This keeps the issues of
assignment visible in the source instead of letting redefinition change values
silently.}  Finally, we specify the behavior of assignment, the operation that
forced us to introduce the environment model in the first place.  The edition's
assignment updates a cell: evaluating an update such as
@code{Ref.set(⟨@var{cell}⟩, ⟨@var{value}⟩)} in some environment locates the
binding of the cell in the environment and changes the contents that binding
indicates.  That is, one finds the first frame in the environment that contains
a binding for the cell and modifies what that frame's binding holds; the frame
that survives is exactly the one the capturing procedure points at.  If the
variable is unbound in the environment, then the program does not compile: the
model's error for assignment to an unbound variable is, in TypeScript, a
compile-time error, and binding never happens by assignment.

These evaluation rules, though considerably more complex than the substitution
model, are still reasonably straightforward.  Moreover, the evaluation model,
though abstract, provides a correct description of how a TypeScript program
evaluates expressions.  In @ref{Chapter 4} we shall see how this model can
serve as a blueprint for implementing a working interpreter.  The following
sections elaborate the details of the model by analyzing some illustrative
programs.

@node	3.2.2, 3.2.3, 3.2.1, 3.2
@subsection Applying Simple Procedures

When we introduced the substitution model in @ref{1.1.5} we showed how
the combination @code{f(5)} evaluates to 136, given the following procedure
definitions:

@example typescript
function square(x: number): number @{
  return x * x;
@}

const sumOfSquares = (x: number, y: number): number =>
  square(x) + square(y);

const f = (a: number): number => sumOfSquares(a + 1, a * 2);
@end example

@noindent
We can analyze the same example using the environment model.  @ref{Figure 3.4}
shows the three procedure objects created by evaluating the definitions of
@code{f}, @code{square}, and @code{sumOfSquares} in the global environment.
Each procedure object consists of some code, together with a pointer to the
global environment.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.4}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.4:} Procedure objects in the global frame.

@example
