global -->|                                                     |
env       +-----------------------------------------------------+
            ^              ^                ^               ^
(f 5)       |              |                |               |
        +------+       +-------+        +------+        +-------+
  E1 -->| a: 5 |  E2 ->| x: 6  |  E3 -->| x: 6 |  E4 -->| x: 10 |
        |      |       | y: 10 |        |      |        |       |
        +------+       +-------+        +------+        +-------+
   sum-of-squares(   square(x) +       x·x             x·x
     a + 1,            square(y)
     2·a)
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.5b,,142mm,Figure 3.5: Environments created by evaluating f(5) using the procedures in Figure 3.4.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.5b.std,147mm,,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.5:} Environments created by evaluating @code{f(5)} @w{using} the procedures in @ref{Figure 3.4}.}
@end float
@noindent
To evaluate this combination, we first evaluate the subexpressions.  The first
subexpression, @code{sumOfSquares}, has a value that is a procedure object.
(Notice how this value is found: We first look in the first frame of E1, which
contains no binding for @code{sumOfSquares}.  Then we proceed to the
enclosing environment, i.e. the global environment, and find the binding shown
in @ref{Figure 3.4}.)  The other two subexpressions are evaluated by applying
the primitive operations @code{+} and @code{*} to evaluate the two combinations
@code{a + 1} and @code{a * 2} to obtain 6 and 10, respectively.

@example typescript
f(5)
// => 136
@end example

@noindent
Now we apply the procedure object @code{sumOfSquares} to the arguments 6 and
10.  This results in a new environment E2 in which the formal parameters
@code{x} and @code{y} are bound to the arguments.  Within E2 we evaluate the
combination @code{square(x) + square(y)}.  This leads us to evaluate
@code{square(x)}, where @code{square} is found in the global frame and
@code{x} is 6.  Once again, we set up a new environment, E3, in which @code{x}
is bound to 6, and within this we evaluate the body of @code{square}, which is
@code{x * x}.  Also as part of applying @code{sumOfSquares}, we must
evaluate the subexpression @code{square(y)}, where @code{y} is 10.  This
second call to @code{square} creates another environment, E4, in which
@code{x}, the formal parameter of @code{square}, is bound to 10.  And within E4
we must evaluate @code{x * x}.

The important point to observe is that each call to @code{square} creates a new
environment containing a binding for @code{x}.  We can see here how the
different frames serve to keep separate the different local variables all named
@code{x}.  Notice that each frame created by @code{square} points to the global
environment, since this is the environment indicated by the @code{square}
procedure object.

After the subexpressions are evaluated, the results are returned.  The values
generated by the two calls to @code{square} are added by @code{sumOfSquares},
and this result is returned by @code{f}.  Since our focus here is on the
environment structures, we will not dwell on how these returned values are
passed from call to call; however, this is also an important aspect of the
evaluation process, and we will return to it in detail in @ref{Chapter 5}.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.9}Exercise 3.9:} @emph{This edition replaces SICP exercise 3.9 with a host-language
exercise that keeps its number and teaching objective.}  In @ref{1.2.1} we used the
substitution model to analyze two procedures for computing factorials, a
recursive version

@example typescript
const factorialRecursive = (n: number): number =>
  n === 1 ? 1 : n * factorialRecursive(n - 1);
@end example

@noindent
and an iterative version

@example typescript
const factorialIter = (n: number): number => @{
  let product = 1;
  let counter = 1;
  while (counter <= n) @{
    product = counter * product;
    counter += 1;
  @}
  return product;
@};
@end example

Draw the stack of frames live at the deepest point of evaluating
@code{factorialRecursive(6)}, and show that @code{factorialIter(6)} never has
more than one frame live at a time.  Then instrument both versions with a
depth recorder that reports the greatest number of frames the run held at
once, and check your drawing: the recursive version should reach a depth of 6,
one frame per call from 6 down to 1, while the iterative version should never
leave depth 1.@footnote{The environment
model will not clarify our claim in @ref{1.2.1} that the interpreter
can execute a procedure such as @code{factorialIter} in a constant amount of space
using tail recursion.  TypeScript inherits JavaScript's engines, and they make
no tail-call promise: the 2015 language specification described proper tail
calls, but the most widely used engine never shipped them, so a self-call in
tail position is still a new stack frame, and deep recursion can exhaust the
stack.  The loop is the only one of the two versions whose frame count is
guaranteed.  We will discuss how an interpreter can supply the guarantee for
every recursive call in @ref{5.4}.}
@end quotation

@node	3.2.3, 3.2.4, 3.2.2, 3.2
@subsection Frames as the Repository of Local State

We can turn to the environment model to see how procedures and assignment can
be used to represent objects with local state.  As an example, consider the
``withdrawal processor'' from @ref{3.1.1} created by calling the
procedure

@example typescript
const makeWithdraw = (initialBalance: number): WithdrawalProcessor => @{
  const encapsulated = Ref.makeUnsafe(initialBalance);
  return (amount) =>
    Effect.gen(function* () @{
      const current = yield* Ref.get(encapsulated);
      if (current < amount) @{
        return yield* new InsufficientFunds();
      @}
      return yield* Ref.setAndGet(encapsulated, current - amount);
    @});
@};
@end example

@noindent
Let us describe the evaluation of

@example typescript
const w1 = makeWithdraw(100);
@end example

@noindent
followed by

@example typescript
Effect.runSync(w1(50))
// => 50
@end example

@noindent
@ref{Figure 3.6} shows the result of defining the @code{makeWithdraw}
procedure in the global environment.  This produces a procedure object that
contains a pointer to the global environment.  So far, this is no different
from the examples we have already seen, except that the body of the procedure
builds and returns another function value, one whose body names a binding,
@code{encapsulated}, that belongs to the call that created it.  The binding of
the cell is what the model calls the environment part of the returned
processor: the processor keeps the cell alive, because a function value keeps
alive every binding its body names, and the cell must outlive the call that
made it.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.6}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 3.6:} Result of defining @code{make-withdrawer} in the global environment.

@example
          +---------------------------+
global -->| make-withdrawer: --+      |
env       +------------------|--------+
                             |      ^
                             V      |
                         .---.---.  |
                         | O | O-+--+
                         `-|-^---'
                           |
                           V
         parameters: balance
         body: given amount
               if balance >= amount
               then balance := balance - amount;
                    return balance
               else "insufficient funds"
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.6c,,128mm,Figure 3.6: Result of defining make-withdrawer in the global environment.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.6c.std,147mm,,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.6:} Result of defining @code{make-withdrawer} in the global environment.}
@end float
@noindent
The interesting part of the computation happens when we apply the procedure
@code{makeWithdraw} to an argument:

@example typescript
const w1 = makeWithdraw(100);
@end example

@noindent
We begin, as usual, by setting up an environment E1 in which the formal
parameter @code{initialBalance} is bound to the argument 100.  Within this
environment, we evaluate the body of @code{makeWithdraw}.  The
@code{Ref.makeUnsafe} call creates the balance cell inside E1, and evaluating
the arrow function constructs a new procedure object, whose code is the text
of the arrow and whose environment is E1, the environment in which the arrow
was evaluated to produce the procedure.  In TypeScript, E1 is a concrete
thing: it is the set of bindings the returned processor keeps alive, and its
resident is the cell.  The resulting procedure object is the value returned by
the call to @code{makeWithdraw}.  It is bound to @code{w1} in the frame that
runs the binding, here the module's top-level frame.
@ref{Figure 3.7} shows the resulting environment structure.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.7}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.7:} Result of evaluating @code{W1 = make-withdrawer(100)}.

@example
