          | make-withdrawer: ---------------------+       |
global -->|                                       |       |
          | W1: --+                               |       |
          +-------|-------------------------------|-------+
                  |                ^              |     ^
                  |                |              V     |
                  |        +-------+------+   .---.---. |
                  |  E1 -->| balance: 100 |   | O | O-+-+
                  |        +--------------+   `-|-^---'
                  V                ^            |
              .---.---.            |            V
            +-+-O | O-+------------+    parameters: balance
            | `---^---'                 body: ...
            V
    parameters: amount
    body: given amount
          if balance >= amount
          then balance := balance - amount;
               return balance
          else "insufficient funds"
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.7b,,145mm,Figure 3.7: Result of evaluating W1 = make-withdrawer(100).,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.7b.std,147mm,,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.7:} Result of evaluating @code{W1 = make-withdrawer(100)}.}
@end float
Now we can analyze what happens when @code{w1} is applied to an argument:

@example typescript
Effect.runSync(w1(50))
// => 50
@end example

@noindent
We begin by constructing a frame in which @code{amount}, the formal parameter
of @code{w1}, is bound to the argument 50.  The essential point to observe is
that this frame has as its enclosing environment not the global environment,
but rather the environment E1, because this is the environment that is
specified by the @code{w1} procedure object; in TypeScript, the call frame
runs a body compiled with access to exactly the bindings the processor
captured, here the cell.  Within this new environment, we
evaluate the body of the procedure:

@example typescript
const current = yield* Ref.get(encapsulated);
if (current < amount) @{
  return yield* new InsufficientFunds();
@}
return yield* Ref.setAndGet(encapsulated, current - amount);
@end example

@noindent
The resulting environment structure is shown in @ref{Figure 3.8}.  The
expression being evaluated references both @code{amount} and
@code{encapsulated}.  @code{Amount} will be found in the first frame in the
environment, while @code{encapsulated} will be found by following the
enclosing-environment pointer to E1, where the cell sits.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.8}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.8:} Environments created by applying the procedure object @code{W1}.

@example
          +---------------------------------------------------+
          | make-withdrawer: ...                              |
global -->|                                                   |
env       | W1: --+                                           |
          +-------|-------------------------------------------+
                  |               ^
                  |               |
                  |       +-------+------+ Here is the balance
                  | E1 -->| balance: 100 | that will be changed
                  |       +--------------+ by the set.
                  V               ^   ^
              .---.---.           |   +----+
              | O | O-+-----------+        |
              `-|-^---'             +------+-----+
                |                   | amount: 50 |
                V                   +------------+
      parameters: amount   if balance >= amount
      body: ...                then balance := balance - amount;
                                    return balance
                               else "insufficient funds"
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.8c,,145mm,Figure 3.8: Environments created by applying the procedure object W1.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.8c.std,147mm,,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.8:} Environments created by applying the procedure object @code{W1}.}
@end float
@noindent
When the @code{Ref.setAndGet} runs, the cell that E1's binding indicates is
changed.  At the completion of the call to @code{w1}, the cell holds 50, and
the frame that contains the binding is still pointed to by the procedure
object @code{w1}.  The frame that binds @code{amount} (in which we executed
the code that changed the cell) is no longer relevant, since the procedure
call that constructed it has terminated, and there are no pointers to that
frame from other parts of the environment.  The next time @code{w1} is called,
this will build a new frame that binds @code{amount} and whose enclosing
environment is E1.  We see that the cell kept alive in E1 serves as the
``place'' that holds the local state variable for the procedure object
@code{w1}.  @ref{Figure 3.9} shows the
situation after the call to @code{w1}.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.9}
